AI researchers, for them, it's like the bigger the computer, the better the AI.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:18,640
You give the researchers the biggest possible computer, even if it is to the detriment of today's profits like the cloud business.

5
00:00:19,054 --> 00:00:24,640
So the compute we have today is a function of decisions I made in 2024.

6
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:36,480
Today, obviously, when you sit and look at anything, everyone is short, not just us, other companies, and everyone wishes you had 2x of what you had planned for.

7
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:40,640
Where's Google's muse or its dots product?

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:48,880
Users, both in consumer and enterprise, will have access to kind of some multi-agent framework for them working all the time doing things.

9
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:54,080
And so, you know, it feels like a moment of infinite possibilities.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:01:06,720
Alphabet CEO Sundar Pichai joins us to talk about Google's plan to compete on the frontier of AI, the balance between cutting-edge AI research and the company's cloud business and what the future of the interface looks like.

11
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,680
And Sundar is here with us in studio in Mountain View today.

12
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:11,280
Sundar, great to see you.

13
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:12,000
Welcome to the show.

14
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:12,960
Thanks, Alex.

15
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:13,840
Great to be here.

16
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:17,700
So Google is back on the frontier with Gemini 4 Argonne.

17
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:21,040
In plain speak, that means it is a world-leading model.

18
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,400
It's benchmark breaking.

19
00:01:22,574 --> 00:01:31,460
It beats OpenAI and Anthropic's public models on a variety of benchmarks, including on coding, math, science, and knowledge work.

20
00:01:31,886 --> 00:01:44,516
Now, the conventional wisdom had been that Google had basically given up on the frontier that it was going to make these fast models that were good enough and use them in its products, and it wasn't pursuing frontier AI anymore.

21
00:01:44,974 --> 00:01:45,668
But that was wrong.

22
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:49,120
I mean, very wrong.

23
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:53,680
Look, I think we are so committed to being at the frontier.

24
00:01:53,870 --> 00:01:55,748
I think it's in our DNA.

25
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:57,764
we won't do it any other way.

26
00:01:59,630 --> 00:02:04,804
we've been thinking about the AI frontier for well over a decade now.

27
00:02:05,934 --> 00:02:12,452
We definitely the frontier is very dynamic, it's very competitive, it's very exciting.

28
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:17,920
I couldn't be more pleased with how uh Argon has come together.

29
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:19,520
We took our time with it.

30
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:25,840
you know, we made sure we tested it safely and deployed it carefully.

31
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:29,860
we took a bit longer than our other models given its capabilities.

32
00:02:30,702 --> 00:02:43,044
But I think across the board, be it for agentic work, knowledge work, coding, creative tasks, I think Argon is you know one of the really good frontier models.

33
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:56,560
And you know, in the first 72 hours since we launched, it's uh you know almost up to 25 trillion tokens used, which is about 3x what Gemini 3.0 had in the first 72 hours.

34
00:02:57,422 --> 00:03:00,400
So uh and we are capacity constrained as we are serving it.

35
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:12,900
So excited, but there are always areas, you know, both the frontier is just last, it's very dynamic and you have to keep evolving the models.

36
00:03:13,358 --> 00:03:17,920
And there are areas which we can see we need to improve as well, so we'll be at work on all of that as well.

37
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:18,884
Which areas?

38
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:23,200
Look, on any given model

39
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:32,996
you know, organ will be across the board better at everything if you just look even two months back.

40
00:03:33,582 --> 00:03:36,036
But the space is so evolving.

41
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:42,788
But even on coding, for example, there are areas pockets where we know we need to do better.

42
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:47,440
and you know, there's clear feedback, and so we'll make it better.

43
00:03:47,918 --> 00:03:52,080
Let me at least make the argument for why standard models might be good enough for Google.

44
00:03:52,558 --> 00:03:54,640
I'm gonna channel what people say about this.

45
00:03:55,918 --> 00:04:08,964
and that is that the frontier of AI today is getting so good and the baseline of AI is getting so good that the frontier is really only valuable for a specific set of tasks.

46
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:13,600
And if you have a standard model, you could actually build great products with a standard model.

47
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,040
Take MetaMuse, for example.

48
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:19,920
Meta built Muse on a good model, but not the best models.

49
00:04:20,462 --> 00:04:26,080
You know, if you need to do like biological research, maybe the frontier is really necessary.

50
00:04:26,286 --> 00:04:36,560
But the people who would say, Google doesn't really need the frontier, they would say you just need those standard models and you can put them in products like maps and Gmail and search.

51
00:04:36,782 --> 00:04:40,452
And um, you know, again, take that meta example, that would be sufficient.

52
00:04:41,134 --> 00:04:42,084
What do you think about that?

53
00:04:44,430 --> 00:04:49,152
Look, it's definitely not how I have thought about it at all.

54
00:04:50,350 --> 00:05:01,072
I think we are deeply committed to driving the state of the art and actually push what this technology can go, where it can go, all the way from Transformers.

55
00:05:01,152 --> 00:05:03,108
That's a journey we've been on.

56
00:05:03,726 --> 00:05:05,892
So you will see us continue to do that.

57
00:05:06,382 --> 00:05:18,660
I think when you're at the frontier, you get an earlier look into the kind of emerging capabilities earlier than others, which helps you better prepare for the future.

58
00:05:19,822 --> 00:05:26,180
We are fortunate to have a wide set of opportunities across consumer and enterprise.

59
00:05:26,702 --> 00:05:31,748
Some of our cloud customers for sure want the model of the frontier.

60
00:05:32,398 --> 00:05:40,352
I think the frontier models will be better at developing the next set of models in an era of recursive self-improvement.

61
00:05:40,622 --> 00:05:42,276
So I would argue it matters there.

62
00:05:42,992 --> 00:05:51,076
Internally, we have definitely seen a steep point of inflection by deploying Argon to our developers.

63
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:56,388
It definitely tackles problems which our previous models could not do.

64
00:05:56,672 --> 00:06:05,028
And so being at that frontier puts you at the innovation frontier and helps you drive that as well.

65
00:06:05,998 --> 00:06:08,992
There is room for models of all sizes and capabilities.

66
00:06:09,134 --> 00:06:26,512
We've always, that's why we did flash, flashlight, flash, et cetera, And I agree with you that you can develop very good product experiences with not necessarily the most frontier model because they tend to be slower and more expensive too.

67
00:06:26,752 --> 00:06:35,072
So I think I think, but I we care about the Pareto Frontier, and so we are committed to doing all of that well.

68
00:06:35,566 --> 00:06:38,672
You said that developing the frontier gives you a look into what's coming.

69
00:06:38,832 --> 00:06:38,992
Yeah.

70
00:06:39,182 --> 00:06:40,192
What do you see on the horizon?

71
00:06:41,934 --> 00:06:45,444
you know, the exciting, what makes this moment so exciting is

72
00:06:49,070 --> 00:06:53,796
you build something which you think is a culmination of like months of work and you put it out.

73
00:06:55,072 --> 00:06:58,512
You know you already see clear headroom ahead, right?

74
00:06:58,672 --> 00:06:59,312
I think.

75
00:07:00,430 --> 00:07:08,004
I mean the frontier is going to, we've all been working on these age and tick long horizon capabilities.

76
00:07:08,718 --> 00:07:14,912
But clearly the frontier is going to have multiple of these agents working together to do things, right?

77
00:07:14,992 --> 00:07:21,232
And and so that's an example of an exciting paradigm underway.

78
00:07:22,894 --> 00:07:33,764
and it's also clear to me that users, both in consumer and enterprise, will have access to kind of some multi-agent framework for them working all the time doing tanks.

79
00:07:34,352 --> 00:07:39,076
And so it feels like a moment of infinite possibilities.

80
00:07:39,918 --> 00:07:41,952
You mentioned recursive self-improvement.

81
00:07:42,112 --> 00:07:45,552
This is the models effectively making themselves better.

82
00:07:46,190 --> 00:07:47,652
Maybe without humans in the loop.

83
00:07:48,032 --> 00:07:58,352
Um people call it like an intelligence explosion or expect to hear this sound like foom, and then all of a sudden AI is just like this superhuman uh capability.

84
00:07:58,512 --> 00:08:03,552
And in fact, uh I think Sergey was um doing this roundtable with researchers at DeepMind.

85
00:08:03,712 --> 00:08:14,116
Yeah, and he talked about with Argon, um, that the core uh one of the core innovations for Argon was relying more on the model to help develop the model, right?

86
00:08:14,672 --> 00:08:19,108
Um we've seen what's gone on with AI models recently.

87
00:08:20,462 --> 00:08:23,072
they are well aligned, but they're not perfectly aligned.

88
00:08:23,182 --> 00:08:25,476
And the imperfections are a big problem.

89
00:08:26,222 --> 00:08:37,072
obviously the tech world, and now the world more broadly knows about the Hugging Face incident where open AIs bots broke containment and hacked into Hugging Face and did some crazy stuff.

90
00:08:37,232 --> 00:08:42,032
Google has also had a scenario where it bots uh got access.

91
00:08:42,352 --> 00:08:45,540
I think they were guessing passwords and they got access to a site they shouldn't have.

92
00:08:45,872 --> 00:08:46,672
They stopped.

93
00:08:46,912 --> 00:09:06,340
But these models, there's enough evidence that they're not doing exactly what we want them to be doing, that this idea of pursuing this recursive self improvement or even making the models better much faster it's sort of freaky.

94
00:09:06,592 --> 00:09:07,684
It makes me scared.

95
00:09:08,846 --> 00:09:17,152
don't you think there's reasonable worry here and there should be something done to have the tech industry chill out about this?

96
00:09:18,734 --> 00:09:21,408
Well, maybe three parts to your question.

97
00:09:21,902 --> 00:09:23,808
first let's talk about RSI itself.

98
00:09:23,968 --> 00:09:34,404
And you know, you're right, Sergey mentioned RSI being a critical contributor as we are developing Oregon.

99
00:09:35,118 --> 00:09:44,688
And but to be very clear, today, you know, in a continuum of we are pretty far from uh you know the end state of RSI or something like that, right?

100
00:09:44,768 --> 00:09:54,928
So today you have the the right way to describe it is you have RSI loops along with AI researchers in various aspects of model development, right?

101
00:09:55,168 --> 00:10:05,668
So, you know, I would say you almost have agents helping AI researchers in some automated way work together to build these models.

102
00:10:06,478 --> 00:10:15,556
So I think it's good to at least understand where we are today, and it's super exciting, and I expect that continuum to keep making progress as we go into next year.

103
00:10:17,422 --> 00:10:19,812
the second question is around safety.

104
00:10:20,334 --> 00:10:26,180
Look, the way we are, I mean, it's a safe security comes from a difference in depth.

105
00:10:27,374 --> 00:10:28,608
You're talking about alignment.

106
00:10:28,768 --> 00:10:37,028
Obviously, you know, we all are doing a lot of work to make sure the models are as aligned as possible.

107
00:10:37,646 --> 00:10:40,420
But it's an active area of research and there are clear gaps.

108
00:10:40,942 --> 00:10:42,928
But that's not the only thing we rely on, right?

109
00:10:43,008 --> 00:10:53,892
So you have the work you do on alignment, but beyond that, you know, we we put a lot of effort into real-time monitoring as these models are working.

110
00:10:54,254 --> 00:11:03,844
So making sure you have good telemetry into what's happening and so that you can intervene in real time if you see unexpected behaviors.

111
00:11:04,398 --> 00:11:15,168
And how you do that, the architecture with which you do that, how do you make sure it's all sandboxed and isolated in a way that it can continue doing its job during real-time monitoring?

112
00:11:15,328 --> 00:11:19,648
These are all areas of investment we are doing.

113
00:11:19,728 --> 00:11:32,848
And obviously, when we deploy these models, giving customers, enterprises control, both with real-time monitoring, but their ability to decide where they deploy, what type of data these models ever have access to, et cetera.

114
00:11:33,038 --> 00:11:38,148
So all that work is very, very important, and we are doing a lot of that with uh with our gon.

115
00:11:39,758 --> 00:11:51,620
So I think as an industry, we need to constantly up our game and take the time to do all this well, including dedicating the compute required to make sure you can safely deploy these models.

116
00:11:52,750 --> 00:11:57,380
The third part is, you know, is it all enough?

117
00:11:57,568 --> 00:12:16,048
And you know, so I think this is why I think, you know, there's a lot of conversations in industry, and I think it's reasonable to be worried, but you know, I won't the right way to approach something you worry about is early on thinking about what more we can do.

118
00:12:16,128 --> 00:12:29,124
And I think all of us are, you you know, I think as an industry, I think you're definitely seeing an increased levels of work on alignment, testing for safety, et cetera, as we deploy these models.

119
00:12:30,254 --> 00:12:42,128
I think the next step would be developing better standards so there are cross-industry standards of how we do these things, including ability to evaluate using independent evaluators.

120
00:12:42,528 --> 00:12:57,648
if you look at areas like financial controls, we have accounting standards, we have independent auditors, et cetera, So those are all the next natural steps for us as an industry to work towards this, and regulation will end up playing a role as we go through all of that as well.

121
00:12:58,048 --> 00:12:59,684
Okay, technology question for you.

122
00:13:01,454 --> 00:13:12,740
Google is somewhat unique in that you research the language or the LLM side of the equation, which started with the transformer language, and then you also pursue world models.

123
00:13:13,408 --> 00:13:22,340
And, you know, there are some AI research efforts out there, like OpenAI, that have basically determined that the LLM is the path to AGI.

124
00:13:22,574 --> 00:13:29,764
And they've actually subset part of their world model research, which is like the AI model that tries to understand the physical world.

125
00:13:30,208 --> 00:13:31,364
but you're pursuing both.

126
00:13:31,886 --> 00:13:36,772
So talk a little bit about why you find the world model side to be important.

127
00:13:39,470 --> 00:13:55,376
You know, I mean, as humans, you obviously perceive the world around you, and you're taking all those inputs, you know, making sure these models are able to understand the laws of physics, uh, simulate the world in high fidelity.

128
00:13:55,536 --> 00:13:59,616
All that, I think, are key enabling blocks for intelligence.

129
00:13:59,776 --> 00:14:07,536
And I think as you're working, we are working across all possible applications of this, you know, take an area like robotics.

130
00:14:07,616 --> 00:14:14,656
It's not an accident argon, if you look at you know, embodied reasoning benchmarks, argon is state of the art, right?

131
00:14:14,816 --> 00:14:17,412
I think literally sort on many of these benchmarks.

132
00:14:18,016 --> 00:14:23,108
And that all comes from our experience building world models as well.

133
00:14:23,376 --> 00:14:30,564
So inherently, I would expect, you know, there will be architectures which will converge all of this in the future.

134
00:14:31,246 --> 00:14:44,656
But I think it's if for us we view this as today, um, you know, in this jagged frontier that is intelligence, we want to be at the frontier of all the possible approaches.

135
00:14:44,736 --> 00:14:56,256
And I think that will help us both drive better research, better insights, and develop a much better uh you know AGI model, uh, you know, is how we think about it.

136
00:14:56,750 --> 00:15:02,930
Yeah, I looked at your Twitter after the Argonne announcement, and it's all just like real world application.

137
00:15:03,054 --> 00:15:07,376
It's not like text or a model generating text.

138
00:15:07,536 --> 00:15:16,496
Okay, I want to talk to you about the way that you balance allocating compute, which is your most precious resource, between cloud and AI research.

139
00:15:16,782 --> 00:15:18,496
That's a big topic about Google.

140
00:15:18,976 --> 00:15:31,136
I've had people suggest to me that Google should get out of the AI research game and take all of its cloud computing resources and its chips and just sell that.

141
00:15:31,216 --> 00:15:39,856
And you look at the world of business right now, And NVIDIA, which is doing that effectively, uh, five trillion dollar company.

142
00:15:40,096 --> 00:15:51,364
Um, there's a push and pull between cloud that wants to, you know, sell these these this computing resource at a high markup, uh, and AI research that needs a very big computer to make the best AI.

143
00:15:51,616 --> 00:15:55,044
Now you've said a big part of your job is figuring out how to allocate compute.

144
00:15:55,456 --> 00:15:58,372
Um how do you do this and how do you balance these two?

145
00:15:59,854 --> 00:16:12,740
Look, I think we are um look uh the ground zero for us is making sure we are investing in the compute that's needed to be at the AI frontier.

146
00:16:13,422 --> 00:16:19,936
So that is, you know, in 2015 I said the company to be AI first.

147
00:16:20,096 --> 00:16:29,540
So AI is that key horizontal ingredient across everything we do, all the way from search to cloud to Waymo to everything we do.

148
00:16:30,126 --> 00:16:38,596
So I think that is ground zero for us, making sure both for research and developing the models that we have enough compute needed.

149
00:16:39,758 --> 00:16:44,976
Beyond that, look, we have these independent businesses which each have their own long-range plans.

150
00:16:45,198 --> 00:16:50,500
And so they're using those long-range plans to project forward, and we plan for compute for all of them.

151
00:16:51,616 --> 00:17:01,316
And so the cloud team, you know, they have a business and they have a projection and they have demand signals, they've committed contracts.

152
00:17:01,742 --> 00:17:04,996
So with that help, we can plan on a long-term compute trajectory for them.

153
00:17:07,438 --> 00:17:10,656
I think we're fortunate to have this wide set of opportunities.

154
00:17:10,816 --> 00:17:14,660
I think there's no company in the world which isn't making trade-offs at any given moment in time.

155
00:17:15,566 --> 00:17:21,060
But the first principles we operate on is to making sure we have enough compute to invest in the frontier.

156
00:17:21,776 --> 00:17:26,016
And that's non negotiable and everything else works on top of it.

157
00:17:26,096 --> 00:17:26,576
Right.

158
00:17:27,246 --> 00:17:33,604
I think what you're seeing in these conversations is if you sit now, remember we are building our own compute, our own data centers.

159
00:17:34,926 --> 00:17:36,164
So there's long lead times.

160
00:17:36,654 --> 00:17:42,020
So the compute we have today is a function of decisions I made in 2024.

161
00:17:43,662 --> 00:17:48,868
Even if you think back to a year from today, last year, the agent recording takeoff hadn't happened.

162
00:17:49,678 --> 00:18:02,064
So today, obviously, when you sit and look at anything, everyone is short, not just us, other companies, and everyone wishes you had 2x of what you had planned for.

163
00:18:02,350 --> 00:18:04,224
I think that comes with the territory, right?

164
00:18:04,304 --> 00:18:10,020
And like you know, at any given moment in time, we are planning for what we would need two to three years out in the future.

165
00:18:10,926 --> 00:18:17,904
So I think you're going to find some decisions you make may look suboptimal two years out, right?

166
00:18:17,984 --> 00:18:20,144
Depending on where the state of the art is.

167
00:18:20,864 --> 00:18:24,624
So that, you know, I don't spend too much time thinking about it.

168
00:18:24,784 --> 00:18:34,864
The way I think about compute allocation is making sure Google DeepMind, both for research and frontier model development, is a cutting edge in terms of compute access, right?

169
00:18:35,184 --> 00:18:38,276
And after that, we plan for all our businesses.

170
00:18:38,894 --> 00:18:44,356
I think it's great we can provide our compute to other people too, like we have we have done.

171
00:18:44,718 --> 00:18:57,344
It allows us to scale that up, get real tests for it in the widest application set possible, you know, test it in the real world and make sure we are at the cutting edge of technology at every layer of our AI stack as well.

172
00:18:58,414 --> 00:19:06,144
You know, briefly, there was a podcast where Ben Horowitz relayed that Andy Jassy had said something like, Computer sold out till 2028.

173
00:19:06,478 --> 00:19:07,652
Are you seeing the same thing?

174
00:19:08,224 --> 00:19:22,064
Um You know, I think it's a, yeah, it's a reasonable, like, you know, if today a customer is coming out and asking us for compute in 27, that is incredibly difficult to do.

175
00:19:22,304 --> 00:19:31,504
Um, you know, there there is uh part of the reason even last year we went out and leased some short-term compute was to accommodate some customers and help them out.

176
00:19:31,984 --> 00:19:36,388
but yes, yeah, I think we are all dealing with similar, timelines.

177
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:38,852
one bubble question.

178
00:19:39,694 --> 00:19:44,516
there's talk of how every technology shift has seen overbuilding.

179
00:19:46,222 --> 00:19:47,664
railroads, fiber.

180
00:19:49,230 --> 00:19:51,108
do you think there's overbuilding here?

181
00:19:53,486 --> 00:19:55,424
It depends on the time frames you're looking at.

182
00:19:56,142 --> 00:20:03,824
are we you know, this level of build, you know, if I take a long enough time frame, it makes complete sense to me.

183
00:20:03,984 --> 00:20:07,664
We are at early stages of the most consequential technology ever.

184
00:20:07,984 --> 00:20:14,340
And this is not like fiber, it's not dark, we are using it, we are capacity constrained on a daily basis.

185
00:20:15,822 --> 00:20:19,940
the applications, the underlying technology is improving rapidly, right?

186
00:20:20,864 --> 00:20:36,100
But running a company, the way I work through the moment is you look at your demand signals, you look at your committed revenue opportunities that you're getting in the forms of contracts.

187
00:20:37,102 --> 00:20:41,824
you balance that with the underlying technical capability, our internal evals, how we are using it.

188
00:20:41,824 --> 00:20:43,652
internally, the model trajectories.

189
00:20:44,270 --> 00:20:51,748
And based on all that, you take a disciplined approach, a confident, aggressive, but you know, a disciplined approach to plan out compute.

190
00:20:52,878 --> 00:21:02,624
But when you talk about the broader economy as a whole, you're having people operate with various levels of risk.

191
00:21:02,704 --> 00:21:10,276
And and so, you know, so it's tough for me to I focus on what I can control as a company and plan through it.

192
00:21:11,424 --> 00:21:24,704
If your question is, can there be an overbuild, Yes, because you know you can have some constrained components, and remember, what matters is a square set of everything you need to build out that compute.

193
00:21:25,198 --> 00:21:27,024
Memory, electricity, electricians,

194
00:21:27,024 --> 00:21:27,904
yeah, like plans.

195
00:21:28,224 --> 00:21:31,024
There are like 2,000 components or more, right?

196
00:21:31,184 --> 00:21:35,184
And so you could get constrained at some point.

197
00:21:35,470 --> 00:21:40,280
There could be things which cause bottlenecks, and you know, and then you could have overbills.

198
00:21:40,944 --> 00:21:43,984
There could be external shocks, right, to the economy, et cetera.

199
00:21:44,144 --> 00:21:49,508
So there are macro risks, all that is possible at any given time.

200
00:21:50,704 --> 00:21:59,664
But you know, am I confident that the compute we are planning for, we will have uses for it given enough time?

201
00:21:59,904 --> 00:22:00,704
Absolutely, yes.

202
00:22:01,104 --> 00:22:02,464
Okay, one more on this.

203
00:22:03,584 --> 00:22:13,904
you know, if you're going to do both, there's been a line of thinking out there that uh AI researchers, for them, it's like the bigger the computer, the better the AI.

204
00:22:14,190 --> 00:22:19,504
And if you know you're someone, I was in the room when you said that AI would be as profound as fire and electricity.

205
00:22:19,630 --> 00:22:24,464
And uh we all laughed at the time, and I kind of regret that laugh now.

206
00:22:24,558 --> 00:22:38,464
Um but if you're someone that actually believes that, then the thinking would be um you give the researchers the biggest possible computer, even if it is to the detriment of today's profits like the cloud business.

207
00:22:38,638 --> 00:22:39,236
Your thoughts.

208
00:22:41,102 --> 00:22:43,040
We're all doing some form of that.

209
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:43,680
Right.

210
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:59,620
I think our cloud business is at any given moment in time foregoing short-term revenues because we are making compute trade-offs to support frontier AI development.

211
00:23:00,206 --> 00:23:03,652
So I think what you're saying is true already.

212
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:04,240
Right.

213
00:23:06,030 --> 00:23:07,760
but you want to be thoughtful, right?

214
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:13,920
Even our AI researchers, uh, you know, some constraints are also good.

215
00:23:14,222 --> 00:23:15,908
That's what inspires creativity.

216
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:23,524
You know, that's what helps you come with a more efficient algorithmic breakthrough, maybe to use less memory, et cetera.

217
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:33,520
So, you know, so uh what we are committed is to invest what's needed to stay at the frontier, right?

218
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:34,564
That's how I think about it.

219
00:23:35,342 --> 00:23:42,480
I wonder what you think the future of the interface looks like, the way that we use computing in the future as AI starts to roll out.

220
00:23:42,640 --> 00:23:46,240
I was speaking with Greg Brockman, the president of OpenAI, about this a couple months ago.

221
00:23:46,702 --> 00:23:47,812
He had a very interesting quote.

222
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:50,212
he said, What is the interface that you want?

223
00:23:50,926 --> 00:23:52,080
What is the product that you want?

224
00:23:52,334 --> 00:23:54,468
The answer is that you want no interface.

225
00:23:54,670 --> 00:23:55,748
You want no product.

226
00:23:56,110 --> 00:24:00,800
You want this to be what's the interface between you and me, basically saying, just talking.

227
00:24:01,038 --> 00:24:08,240
That if AI is that smart, there shouldn't be like a you know, maybe a screen or just so many buttons and things that you have to touch.

228
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:11,460
what is your view of what the interface looks like in the age of AI?

229
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:22,820
The way I think about it is I do feel you know, computing historically has been humans adapting to computing.

230
00:24:23,694 --> 00:24:26,080
With AI, it'll be the other way about.

231
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:33,760
The technology will adapt seamlessly to how humans live and go about their day-to-day lives.

232
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:35,360
So there's an aspect of it.

233
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:48,800
Um, you know, you want that AI to be able to speak to it when you want it to, it has context, it can be persistently running somewhere, give you continuity.

234
00:24:49,486 --> 00:24:53,924
You're not dealing with a lot of complexity, it's natural, all that makes sense to me.

235
00:24:54,414 --> 00:24:56,800
That doesn't mean it won't have an interface, however, to me.

236
00:24:57,582 --> 00:25:10,468
look today, for example, I mean, nowadays, I just even in my phone, like you know, the Gemini voice models are so good, I can just press the microphone button.

237
00:25:11,022 --> 00:25:12,480
I can just ramble.

238
00:25:12,640 --> 00:25:14,240
That's how you call it rambler, right?

239
00:25:14,350 --> 00:25:15,236
I can ramble.

240
00:25:15,630 --> 00:25:20,000
It kind of intelligently corrects with my personal context.

241
00:25:20,238 --> 00:25:26,980
So even my friends' names, like weird names, it kind of gets it right, even if it first guesses it wrong.

242
00:25:27,662 --> 00:25:28,160
It's amazing.

243
00:25:29,040 --> 00:25:34,240
I'm not going in backspace editing once I finish saying it, right?

244
00:25:34,320 --> 00:25:36,068
It's remarkable how good it's gotten.

245
00:25:37,230 --> 00:25:39,280
But there are going to be moments you will need an interface.

246
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:47,044
You may want to entertain yourself, you may want to watch a video, so you may ask your AI to create something and you'll want to watch it, consume it, right?

247
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:57,920
So I think the way I think about it is you want it to be as seamless and as natural as possible, and that you are not doing the hard work adapting to whatever the interface is.

248
00:25:58,190 --> 00:26:01,600
The technology is adapting to how you're working at any given moment in time.

249
00:26:02,062 --> 00:26:04,560
Yeah, I'm just going to play the open AI hits.

250
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:08,160
because last year you sent them into a code red.

251
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:09,600
I'm sure you know.

252
00:26:10,510 --> 00:26:15,120
Gemini was doing very well in OpenAI, battened down the hatches.

253
00:26:15,600 --> 00:26:18,532
and I was speaking with Sam Altman about the challenge from Google.

254
00:26:18,960 --> 00:26:25,508
What he said was he thought OpenAI would fend you off because they're building AI native.

255
00:26:25,920 --> 00:26:31,588
And I don't know if he said it specifically like this, but he implied Google is bolting on AI to existing products.

256
00:26:32,174 --> 00:26:35,360
What is your view of whether um, you know, a bolt-on?

257
00:26:35,840 --> 00:26:41,060
I wonder if you would accept that framing, uh, can compete with an AI native product.

258
00:26:42,414 --> 00:26:45,840
Look, first of all, uh I mean it's a very dynamic moment.

259
00:26:45,920 --> 00:26:47,120
We are all pushing each other.

260
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:50,800
I think OpenAI has pushed us out as a company too.

261
00:26:50,880 --> 00:26:54,880
So this is not just you know, we are doing something to them alone, et cetera.

262
00:26:55,054 --> 00:26:55,520
Far from it.

263
00:26:55,840 --> 00:26:57,120
Yeah, you went into code red too.

264
00:26:57,200 --> 00:26:59,000
I mean, I know it's disputed about whether it was, but

265
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:05,920
we've all it's been the AI moment, all of us had to respond with varying levels of urgency at any given moment in time.

266
00:27:06,766 --> 00:27:12,720
Look, I think you know, we are constantly building new products as well.

267
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:17,924
I mean, Gemini was built from the ground up, like as a product, the Gemini app I'm talking about.

268
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:23,716
Or, you know, tomorrow, as all of us are adapting to agents.

269
00:27:25,262 --> 00:27:29,104
Are you bolting agents onto ChatGPD or are they building it natively?

270
00:27:29,184 --> 00:27:30,144
How would you think about that?

271
00:27:30,478 --> 00:27:32,304
So you can always flip these questions back.

272
00:27:33,184 --> 00:27:33,424
Right.

273
00:27:33,584 --> 00:27:35,504
So I don't think of it that way.

274
00:27:35,664 --> 00:27:43,504
I mean, I think of it as you want to build world-class product experiences that are AI native and they are winning.

275
00:27:43,664 --> 00:27:49,184
And some of it is adapting products, some of it is rethinking new experiences.

276
00:27:49,344 --> 00:27:51,984
So they will all be part of how you approach this moment.

277
00:27:52,558 --> 00:27:56,544
Aaron Powell Talk a little bit about the way that you see the business of this AI interface in the future.

278
00:27:56,784 --> 00:28:11,744
So for instance, if I'm asking my agent to go out and do something for me and it suggests a product, should it, I mean, maybe even if disclosed, does it get a cut of the sale if I make the sale?

279
00:28:11,824 --> 00:28:15,812
Or when you think about the way that you're going to make money from these products, what's the plan?

280
00:28:18,574 --> 00:28:24,144
I think, you know, obviously I'm going to put enterprise aside.

281
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:24,840
Okay.

282
00:28:25,184 --> 00:28:25,904
Yeah, consumer question.

283
00:28:26,304 --> 00:28:27,972
so you know, if it if it's consumer.

284
00:28:29,742 --> 00:28:34,464
Look, I think there are consumers are going to increasingly value this technology.

285
00:28:34,544 --> 00:28:38,180
They're gonna meaningfully, I mean, take software developers today.

286
00:28:39,118 --> 00:28:43,584
I think developers are willing to pay for these coding models, right?

287
00:28:43,664 --> 00:28:46,224
And because it really helps makes them more productive.

288
00:28:46,384 --> 00:28:52,004
Nobody would want to code the way they were coding three years ago, or even like same time last year.

289
00:28:53,198 --> 00:28:56,996
So I think when you're delivering value, there's value to be had.

290
00:28:58,574 --> 00:29:12,452
I think history of consumer product shows if you build a consumer product, I've never seen examples where consumer products really scale to billions of users where people are using it intensely and hasn't had monetization to go along with it.

291
00:29:12,910 --> 00:29:17,824
If you're providing value to people, there is inherent value to be had.

292
00:29:18,784 --> 00:29:21,744
And so the nature of it may change, right?

293
00:29:21,824 --> 00:29:25,412
I think you're asking a scenario in which agents are doing stuff for you.

294
00:29:26,222 --> 00:29:29,604
At the end of the day, you will have commercial needs.

295
00:29:30,702 --> 00:29:33,092
The agents need to fulfill those commercial needs.

296
00:29:33,326 --> 00:29:39,904
The providers on the other side are going to see value in that, and there's going to be some mechanism by which it gets fleshed out.

297
00:29:39,984 --> 00:29:43,108
That's what an efficient system does.

298
00:29:43,694 --> 00:29:48,224
But I think what matters is that you're building those world-class consumer experiences, right?

299
00:29:48,464 --> 00:29:53,264
And so um I answering your question, I just want to make sure.

300
00:29:53,504 --> 00:29:54,064
Yeah, definitely.

301
00:29:54,144 --> 00:29:54,544
Yeah.

302
00:29:55,566 --> 00:29:58,784
I promised myself one product feature request and one only.

303
00:29:59,022 --> 00:29:59,264
Yes.

304
00:29:59,424 --> 00:30:00,164
Here it goes.

305
00:30:02,478 --> 00:30:15,344
Is it possible to make search and Gmail powered by Gemini as good as search and Gmail that you access when you plug Gmail into your Chat GPT interface?

306
00:30:15,662 --> 00:30:16,544
What's going on here?

307
00:30:18,510 --> 00:30:35,184
I think you know, we we have one of the exciting things we announced recently is in a unified Gemini agent for enterprise, which we have announced and we're bringing that to consumers, right?

308
00:30:35,264 --> 00:30:44,644
So these are persistent agents which run 24-7 in the cloud, like you're spinning up a micro VM effectively for every query.

309
00:30:46,704 --> 00:30:52,464
and from the runtime, the harness, the model, it's all a headless service, right?

310
00:30:52,544 --> 00:30:54,084
And it'll work everywhere.

311
00:30:54,446 --> 00:30:56,772
And it it has your context with you.

312
00:30:57,966 --> 00:31:06,660
You can address this agent, whether you're in workspace, in search, you know, in Gemini, all of it.

313
00:31:06,864 --> 00:31:13,664
So I think agents are going to drive this unification from a customer experience, which people are looking for.

314
00:31:13,904 --> 00:31:14,064
Yeah.

315
00:31:14,318 --> 00:31:23,424
And you are right, when we when you have a broad product portfolio and you're moving forward fast, there are always areas where you're moving forward faster than some other areas.

316
00:31:23,824 --> 00:31:26,064
that's that's uh valid feedback, right?

317
00:31:26,224 --> 00:31:37,984
And uh but we are putting a lot of time and effort into making sure as we build out our agentic experience that unification and that capability works well across all these surfaces.

318
00:31:38,304 --> 00:31:38,544
Okay.

319
00:31:38,624 --> 00:31:40,384
That's a good answer to the interface question, too.

320
00:31:40,784 --> 00:31:41,664
Yes, it is, yeah.

321
00:31:42,734 --> 00:31:48,464
when people have thought about where Google plays next, a question comes up.

322
00:31:48,558 --> 00:31:52,836
You know, where's Google's Muse or its dots product?

323
00:31:53,344 --> 00:31:57,252
those have kind of come out of nowhere recently and they've taken off.

324
00:31:57,744 --> 00:31:58,916
You have a version of that.

325
00:32:00,878 --> 00:32:07,236
do you anticipate creating a new product that competes with, let's just say Muse, for instance?

326
00:32:09,006 --> 00:32:12,644
look, We already today.

327
00:32:13,040 --> 00:32:23,280
So the way we have envisioned this, in fact, we we describe Gemini, our goal with Gemini app to be a personal, proactive, and powerful assistant, right?

328
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:24,836
That's all that's what we are working towards.

329
00:32:25,582 --> 00:32:30,560
And you saw us describe the early versions of it at Google IO, um, uh Google I.O.

330
00:32:30,720 --> 00:32:31,876
this year with Spark.

331
00:32:32,718 --> 00:32:44,452
But effectively, already, you know, if you open the Gemini app as a pro or an ultra user, you see chat and PASC and and and and they start giving you that agent tick experience.

332
00:32:45,550 --> 00:32:50,020
With ARGON now, that capability is dramatically accelerated.

333
00:32:50,800 --> 00:32:56,388
And what we announced recently in our enterprise event, we are bringing that to consumers as well.

334
00:32:56,942 --> 00:33:03,236
But over time, so you will soon in Gemini you will be able to ask something.

335
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:06,640
And that will, it's a continuum.

336
00:33:06,830 --> 00:33:12,080
You know, you don't need to think about whether you're asking a chat or an uh agency task.

337
00:33:12,398 --> 00:33:20,804
It'll just kind of automatically do the right thing and it can work persistently on the background securely and get things done for you.

338
00:33:21,422 --> 00:33:30,660
Already empirically, when I look at what's coming into Gemini in our app, about last year you would have tagged them and most of them would have been chats.

339
00:33:31,534 --> 00:33:37,200
But a steadily growing percentage is already agentic tasks in Gemini.

340
00:33:37,680 --> 00:33:43,908
And so so, yes, we are going to bring personal agents to our consumers.

341
00:33:44,238 --> 00:33:51,200
It'll work in the Gemini app, it'll work in Google Search, it'll work in Gmail, you can address it, call it, et cetera.

342
00:33:51,342 --> 00:33:53,920
But we want to make sure we're getting it right.

343
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:58,560
It'll be powered by Argon, and so we are very excited to bring it to our users.

344
00:33:58,880 --> 00:34:03,396
I've got that pro subscription I've been tasked, so I'm looking forward to using it more.

345
00:34:04,100 --> 00:34:05,828
All right, a couple of random questions.

346
00:34:06,480 --> 00:34:13,760
Google's committed and invested, invested and has committed tens of billions of dollars to Anthropic.

347
00:34:14,159 --> 00:34:19,235
what do you see in Dario that makes you want to have such a deep commitment and investment to him?

348
00:34:20,159 --> 00:34:26,800
Very early on, when we looked at the founding team of Anthropic, it was very clear to us.

349
00:34:26,960 --> 00:34:33,636
Um, you know, this was a world-class team which was involved in some of the earliest GPD models.

350
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:38,947
And so we were quickly able to assess that, you know, they they would be at the frontier.

351
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:48,480
And so we've always tried to identify and partner with companies which would be at the cutting edge of technology.

352
00:34:48,560 --> 00:34:53,040
We've done that with SpaceX, we've done that with Anthropic, and uh, you know, that's what we saw.

353
00:34:53,199 --> 00:34:56,000
And we had a chance to partner.

354
00:34:56,159 --> 00:35:00,480
Some of the partnerships turned in the construct of our TPUs as well.

355
00:35:00,640 --> 00:35:03,520
So I think that's how we approach it.

356
00:35:03,920 --> 00:35:09,924
Okay, with Apple, you had a partnership with them to license Gemini to them to help improve Siri.

357
00:35:10,478 --> 00:35:13,700
Is that partnership expanding, contracting, standing still?

358
00:35:14,926 --> 00:35:27,120
I mean, we are uh uh look both for both us and Apple, that partnership was about translating the capabilities of AI into better experiences for users.

359
00:35:27,360 --> 00:35:37,840
And uh, you know, I thought Apple has done a phenomenal job of incorporating, they customize these models and build it into Siri.

360
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:40,160
And it's been great to see the reception.

361
00:35:40,320 --> 00:35:42,800
And so we are committed to doing more.

362
00:35:43,310 --> 00:35:44,164
You're going to do more with them.

363
00:35:44,720 --> 00:35:48,836
There's nothing specific to comment, but you know, I mean it's uh it's a partnership that's working well.

364
00:35:49,600 --> 00:35:55,520
Uh data centers in space, you've announced that you have uh you're doing more there.

365
00:35:55,822 --> 00:35:58,640
What have you learned so far about the feasibility of that project?

366
00:35:59,840 --> 00:36:02,560
I mean it's a it's a very exciting project.

367
00:36:02,670 --> 00:36:16,880
You know, projects like this remind me, I still recall that moment in uh in the desert uh for the DARPA Grand Challenge, watching the Stanley car you know, compete in it, which ended up being the basis for Waymo, right.

368
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:31,268
And so you're always, with all these technologies, you're at a moment in time where it looks a bit like sci-fi, but you know, 10 to 20 years down the line, it's actually working in a meaningful way.

369
00:36:31,680 --> 00:36:33,200
I feel the same with space.

370
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:37,040
You know, we've always believed in betting big on these technologies.

371
00:36:37,120 --> 00:36:39,172
Quantum has been a great example of it.

372
00:36:39,920 --> 00:36:41,440
we are probably 10 years into the journey.

373
00:36:41,600 --> 00:36:44,708
Waymo, we are about almost 20 years into the journey.

374
00:36:45,358 --> 00:36:47,920
Space, we are two to three years into the journey, right?

375
00:36:48,080 --> 00:36:54,960
And it's clear to me that it's an approach which will make a lot of sense in the future.

376
00:36:55,200 --> 00:37:09,252
Being in sun-synchronous orbits with 24-7 access to the most abundant form of energy we have access to will make a lot of sense for this exponential increase in compute that we are going to need.

377
00:37:09,742 --> 00:37:12,560
But having said that, I mean, this is a challenging project.

378
00:37:12,720 --> 00:37:19,332
The team has disciplined milestones, and so it was a super exciting milestone to send the TPUs up to space.

379
00:37:19,662 --> 00:37:24,880
And sometime as we are speaking, we are expecting a hello world moment from them.

380
00:37:24,960 --> 00:37:31,684
And so I look forward to seeing that happen, and we'll continue investing here too.

381
00:37:33,040 --> 00:37:39,108
you recently, was it maybe last year, you said that 25% of code written within Google was written by AI.

382
00:37:39,502 --> 00:37:40,388
What's the number today?

383
00:37:41,678 --> 00:37:54,340
I mean, we have far, I think we've almost that number in our world today is like meaningless in the sense that every part of code we now touch is influenced by AI deeply.

384
00:37:54,830 --> 00:38:02,960
What's now what we are tracking with Argonne, for example, we are now able to see it's been a point of inflection.

385
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:08,400
People are using Oregon to really go tackle complex projects, right?

386
00:38:08,560 --> 00:38:12,356
And including projects which affects millions of lines of code.

387
00:38:13,070 --> 00:38:28,800
So we have definitely in the four weeks since we've launched Oregon, we have seen a 30% increase in CLs and more importantly, almost a similar increase, you know, one third, decrease in like rollbacks, right?

388
00:38:28,960 --> 00:38:35,876
And so I think people are writing code more productively and better code with it, uh code with it.

389
00:38:36,160 --> 00:38:40,484
So I think uh I think so we are tracking it with newer metrics now.

390
00:38:41,880 --> 00:38:45,764
You know, talking about the opposition, to data centers, and how do you feel that?

391
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:52,452
and if you can't build data centers in the US the way that you want to, where else do you go?

392
00:38:53,966 --> 00:38:59,280
We're you know, we're very committed to building our data centers very responsibly.

393
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:14,160
I think when I look at our largest data centers in some of the communities where we have been the longest, uh, you know, I I visit them, I talk to people, you know, there are people who would come and tell us their kids have gone to college because of our investments in these communities.

394
00:39:14,350 --> 00:39:17,120
And so, and we've grown in those communities.

395
00:39:17,280 --> 00:39:27,040
And so I think to me, this is about doing it right, you know, being engaging with the communities early, doing it transparently.

396
00:39:27,406 --> 00:39:37,124
We are committed to making sure we are bringing incremental power and we cover 100% of the cost of our power needs for these projects.

397
00:39:37,518 --> 00:39:44,880
It's understanding the impact on neighborhoods and making sure you're working early to address them, et cetera.

398
00:39:44,974 --> 00:39:51,040
So I think done rightly, I think you can continue making progress.

399
00:39:51,280 --> 00:39:57,508
But this is obviously we are constantly taking feedback into account and so on.

400
00:39:57,838 --> 00:40:01,120
I do worry not everyone operates the same way.

401
00:40:01,440 --> 00:40:04,400
Yeah, people have been surprised by data centers showing up in their backyard.

402
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:10,400
So I think those are all important to as an industry, we should continue to do better.

403
00:40:10,862 --> 00:40:23,236
But part of the reason for in a 20-year time frame we are thinking about things like data centers in space is making sure we are able to put these data centers where maybe the trade-offs will be different.

404
00:40:24,080 --> 00:40:28,740
Is there an unproven discipline in AI right now that Google is working hardest to crack?

405
00:40:29,262 --> 00:40:37,028
So people have talked about continuous learning, getting these AIs to continue to update after they get released as opposed to forget through every session.

406
00:40:38,254 --> 00:40:40,048
Look, there are multiple parallel bets.

407
00:40:40,128 --> 00:40:43,876
I mean, you saw with Oregon we have a million output tokens, right?

408
00:40:43,968 --> 00:40:45,092
You know, you know.

409
00:40:45,902 --> 00:40:52,368
Uh with our Gemini models early on, we had a 1 million context window on the input side, right?

410
00:40:52,974 --> 00:40:57,604
These are all examples of breakthroughs we are constantly working on.

411
00:40:58,030 --> 00:41:00,128
You know, how do you think about synthetic data?

412
00:41:00,288 --> 00:41:02,468
How do you think about continual learning?

413
00:41:03,150 --> 00:41:08,528
There are how do you think about more efficient architectures?

414
00:41:08,910 --> 00:41:15,328
So part of we do dedicate a meaningful percentage of our compute to these research bets too.

415
00:41:15,488 --> 00:41:22,768
And so it's always you know, it always goes hand in hand, including our world models, et cetera.

416
00:41:22,848 --> 00:41:23,088
Right.

417
00:41:23,248 --> 00:41:26,608
So those are all bets we are placing on.

418
00:41:26,688 --> 00:41:31,728
Sometimes, as part of these bets, there are ideas that emerge which just make their way into the main lane models.

419
00:41:31,808 --> 00:41:39,168
And so maybe, you don't fully, talk about it.

420
00:41:39,248 --> 00:41:41,540
But I think we have multiple bets in these areas.

421
00:41:42,126 --> 00:41:43,300
Okay, I want to end with this.

422
00:41:44,528 --> 00:41:50,448
to be involved in the tech industry the way that you are, there's a certain inherent optimism to it, right?

423
00:41:50,608 --> 00:41:54,180
A belief that if you build, you will improve the world.

424
00:41:54,606 --> 00:41:57,728
And this is something deep within the tech industry.

425
00:41:57,968 --> 00:41:59,808
Now, there are sides to that, right?

426
00:41:59,888 --> 00:42:09,380
If you it requires betting, oftentimes like betting a lot and hoping that the thing that you're working on is going to come to fruition uh in the way that you want it to be.

427
00:42:09,648 --> 00:42:10,928
but it doesn't always work that way.

428
00:42:11,182 --> 00:42:12,100
I'll give you an example.

429
00:42:12,448 --> 00:42:26,368
Um, I was just visiting El Salvador for the first, well, I I went in a five-year bracket and I saw um in this one town uh just a amazing amount of uh enthusiasm for Bitcoin and the blockchain.

430
00:42:26,510 --> 00:42:33,248
And there was a construction happening and it was booming, and then it didn't happen the way that folks expected.

431
00:42:33,774 --> 00:42:38,528
and this visit was basically a ghost town with a lot of unfinished construction projects.

432
00:42:39,822 --> 00:42:49,700
I think that to do what you do, it requires a type of optimism that says it's worth going for, even if the bets don't pan out.

433
00:42:51,310 --> 00:42:53,168
and there could be consequences there.

434
00:42:53,248 --> 00:43:01,648
So I'd just love to hear your perspective on you spoke about this at your Stanford commencement addressed about the need for optimism, but it's a nuanced form of optimism, right?

435
00:43:01,728 --> 00:43:11,524
So I'd love to hear your perspective on sort of how you maintain that and is it like a pure optimism or is it a couched optimism in a way?

436
00:43:13,070 --> 00:43:27,808
Look, I think I genuinely have a deep conviction that uh you know that you know improving technology and improving access to technology makes people's lives better.

437
00:43:27,968 --> 00:43:30,276
It's been my personal experience in my life.

438
00:43:30,798 --> 00:43:33,988
So out of which comes that deep conviction, optimism.

439
00:43:34,702 --> 00:43:43,968
But with that, I've always felt, you know, humanity's journey is always about working hard to harness that technology to make it work well for society.

440
00:43:44,398 --> 00:43:59,488
So the optimism is not an optimism that you just build whatever you want, you work hard to make sure you're building technology in the service of humanity to make the human condition better.

441
00:43:59,648 --> 00:44:03,968
And that's got to be the defining goal as we are approaching it.

442
00:44:04,128 --> 00:44:07,248
And with that framework, I'm very, very optimistic.

443
00:44:07,488 --> 00:44:14,528
And I think we are in very early stages of the kind of progress that we are going to see on many areas.

444
00:44:14,688 --> 00:44:32,528
You know, we are all talking about areas like healthcare and stuff, but you know, I think a lot of the progress is ahead and the ability to make things in a fundamentally cheaper way on many, many things where things have been getting expensive for a long, long time.

445
00:44:32,942 --> 00:44:36,848
Discovering new materials, et cetera, which really bend the curve.

446
00:44:37,262 --> 00:44:43,328
I think it's tough to step today and understand the impact of all that, but they're going to be very profound.

447
00:44:43,568 --> 00:44:45,968
So I'm very optimistic.

448
00:44:46,768 --> 00:44:53,328
but you know, we have to be rightfully concerned about all the ways this technology can go wrong.

449
00:44:53,408 --> 00:44:57,728
And that's how you make sure you solve those concerns.

450
00:44:57,808 --> 00:44:59,844
So I think they both go hand in hand.

451
00:45:00,494 --> 00:45:02,528
Well, Sundar, I know you don't do many of these.

452
00:45:02,688 --> 00:45:06,928
I really appreciate you spending the time with me today, and it was great to get a chance to see you and speak with you again.

453
00:45:07,248 --> 00:45:07,888
Thanks, Alex.

454
00:45:07,968 --> 00:45:08,528
I appreciate it.

455
00:45:08,608 --> 00:45:08,848
Thanks.

456
00:45:09,320 --> 00:45:09,648
Hi, everybody.

457
00:45:09,728 --> 00:45:14,208
Thanks so much for listening and watching, and we'll see you next time on Big Technology Podcast.

